<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sattar Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sattar Khan was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-constitutional-revolution">Iranian Constitutional Revolution</a>, a former horse dealer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> who organised the city's defence after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-ali-shah-qajar">Mohammad Ali Shah</a> closed the Majlis in 1908. His fighters held Tabriz against royalist forces for about ten months, keeping the constitutional cause alive until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> was taken in 1909. Honoured as Sardar-e Melli, the National Commander, he died in Tehran in 1914.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sattar-khan.jpg" alt="Sattar Khan" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SattarKhanPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sattar Khan was born in Sardar Kandy, of Iranian Azerbaijani origin, the third son of Haj Hasan Bazzaz of the Qaradagh. In his childhood his eldest brother, a highway robber, was executed. After the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> he was jailed for two years in the Narin Qalʿa prison for trying to hide two Caucasian fugitives whom his father had sheltered, and he was later imprisoned again for robbery. He also served in the gendarmerie on the road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoy">Khoy</a> and Marand, rode in the armed escort of the crown prince Mozaffar-al-Din Mirza, and led an auxiliary troop against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> robbers near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Close to the Social Democratic Party, he rose from obscurity to lead the Constitutionalist fighters of the Amirkhiz district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, and by 1907 he was a favoured leader of the rebels. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-ali-shah-qajar">Mohammad Ali Shah</a> shelled the Majles, 40 thousand of the Shah's soldiers were ordered against Tabriz. In June 1908 a High Military Council was set up with Sattar Khan as commander-in-chief and Bagher Khan as his deputy. By October 1908 most cities of Azarbaijan province had been cleared of royalist forces, and the Shah allowed the Majles to reopen.</p><p>The defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> cost the rebels many fighters. The Assembly gave Sattar Khan the title Sardar-e Melli and Bagher Khan the title Salar-e Melli. Committees named after Sattar Khan sprang up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasht">Rasht</a>, Qazvin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> and other cities, and his fame helped bring the Bakhtiyari tribal leaders over to the rebels' side.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In March 1910 Sattar Khan and Bagher Khan set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> with 300 soldiers and were welcomed by crowds on 3 April. Camped in Atabek Park, they refused the order to disarm, and on the night of 7 August 1910 government troops and police under Yeprem Khan surrounded and disarmed them; about 30 fighters were killed and Sattar Khan was wounded in the leg, which left him disabled for the rest of his life. He was buried in the Shah-Abdol-Azim shrine cemetery at Shahr-e Ray, and he is remembered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in poems and songs as a hero of the revolution.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sattar Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Constitutionalists held out in Tabriz under leaders such as <b>Sattar Khan</b>, and in July 1909 forces from Gilan and the Bakhtiari tribes took Tehran and deposed the shah in favour of his young son Ahmad Shah.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Rebels held out in Tabriz under <b>Sattar Khan</b> and by April 1909 had driven out the royalist forces.</blockquote><a href="/movement/persian-constitutional-revolution">Persian Constitutional Revolution · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.07,46.3&amp;year=1897" aria-label="Tabriz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5703 795 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sattar Khan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6153 1065h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6153 1065h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.07,46.3&amp;year=1897">What was here in 1897? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
